1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,825
What is Kubernetes?

2
00:00:07,367 --> 00:00:14,660
Kubernetes is a system that runs and looks after containerized applications for you, on one or many machines.

3
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:18,540
You tell it what you want ("three copies of my web server,

4
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:24,629
reachable on one address, always running") and it works continuously to make reality match:

5
00:00:24,629 --> 00:00:28,376
it starts the containers, restarts them when they crash,

6
00:00:28,376 --> 00:00:34,866
replaces them when a machine dies, spreads traffic across them and updates them without downtime.

7
00:00:34,866 --> 00:00:35,602
An analogy:

8
00:00:35,602 --> 00:00:40,553
Docker is a skilled cook who can prepare any dish (container) you ask for.

9
00:00:40,553 --> 00:00:43,029
Kubernetes is the restaurant manager:

10
00:00:43,029 --> 00:00:49,853
it decides which cook prepares what, notices when a cook goes home sick and hands the work to another,

11
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:57,146
opens more stations when the restaurant is busy, and keeps the menu (your desired state) written on the wall.

12
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:01,934
With one container on one machine, Docker is enough.

13
00:01:01,934 --> 00:01:04,865
Applications rarely stay that small:

14
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,020
And how does it actually work?

15
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:12,144
You never start containers yourself.

16
00:01:12,144 --> 00:01:17,371
You describe the desired state in YAML files and send them to Kubernetes:

17
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:19,277
2.

18
00:01:19,277 --> 00:01:20,503
Simulate a crash:

19
00:01:20,503 --> 00:01:26,558
docker kill stops the container abruptly, as a crash or an out-of-memory kill would.

20
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:31,210
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

21
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:37,237
Exit code 137 means the process was killed (128 + signal 9).

22
00:01:37,237 --> 00:01:39,517
The restart policy is no:

23
00:01:39,517 --> 00:01:46,811
Docker was never told what the container should be doing, only to start it once.

24
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:50,328
Let me try something.

25
00:01:50,328 --> 00:01:51,502
Scale by hand:

26
00:01:51,502 --> 00:01:55,611
a second copy of the web server on the same port.

27
00:01:56,167 --> 00:01:57,665
What should happen?

28
00:01:57,665 --> 00:02:00,266
Two copies serving the same site.

29
00:02:00,266 --> 00:02:01,370
What happened?

30
00:02:01,370 --> 00:02:03,420
port is already allocated:

31
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:06,337
a host port belongs to one container.

32
00:02:06,337 --> 00:02:10,594
With plain Docker you would pick a second port (8081),

33
00:02:10,594 --> 00:02:19,266
then put a load balancer in front, then keep a list of which copy runs where, and update it whenever one dies.

34
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:21,713
Got it.

35
00:02:21,713 --> 00:02:24,465
What do I need to remember?

36
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,350
Docker runs containers; Kubernetes manages containerized applications across machines.

37
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:38,435
You describe the desired state; Kubernetes reconciles reality with it, continuously.

38
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,010
Self-healing, scaling, one address for many copies and zero-downtime updates are what you get.

39
00:02:47,767 --> 00:02:51,574
I keep hearing about Kubernetes architecture.

40
00:02:51,574 --> 00:02:53,181
What is it, really?

41
00:02:53,733 --> 00:03:00,043
A Kubernetes cluster is a group of machines (nodes) with one "brain", the control plane.

42
00:03:00,043 --> 00:03:07,141
The control plane stores what you want, decides where things run and corrects anything that drifts.

43
00:03:07,141 --> 00:03:09,794
The worker nodes run your containers.

44
00:03:09,794 --> 00:03:16,032
Every component is a separate program with one job, and they all talk through one door:

45
00:03:16,032 --> 00:03:17,108
the API server.

46
00:03:17,667 --> 00:03:23,703
When something goes wrong in Kubernetes, the symptom tells you which component to look at,

47
00:03:23,703 --> 00:03:26,386
but only if you know what each one does.

48
00:03:26,386 --> 00:03:31,416
A Pod that stays Pending forever, a Pod that is scheduled but never starts,

49
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:34,166
a cluster where nothing changes any more:

50
00:03:34,166 --> 00:03:36,648
each points at a different component.

51
00:03:36,648 --> 00:03:38,526
This lesson builds that map.

52
00:03:40,267 --> 00:03:42,686
And how does it actually work?

53
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:52,379
| Component | Where | Its one job (beginner version) | |---|---|---| | API server (kube-apiserver) | control plane | the front door:

54
00:03:52,379 --> 00:03:59,239
every command (kubectl), every component, talks only to it | | etcd | control plane | the database:

55
00:03:59,239 --> 00:04:16,145
stores the desired and current state of every object | | Scheduler (kube-scheduler) | control plane | picks a node for every new Pod that has none | | Controller manager (kube-controller-manager) | control plane | runs the reconciliation loops:

56
00:04:16,145 --> 00:04:21,826
"3 copies wanted, 2 running → create 1" | | kubelet | every node | the node agent:

57
00:04:21,826 --> 00:04:41,088
starts the Pod's containers, reports their status | | kube-proxy | every node | makes Service addresses (lesson 10) work on the node | | Container runtime (containerd) | every node | actually runs containers (what Docker did on your laptop) | What happens when you create a Pod:

58
00:04:42,833 --> 00:04:43,015
2.

59
00:04:43,015 --> 00:04:43,834
The node:

60
00:04:43,834 --> 00:04:53,024
One node, Ready, with the role control-plane, running Kubernetes v1.37.0 with the containerd runtime.

61
00:04:53,024 --> 00:04:53,206
