76
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:08,010
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

77
00:06:08,567 --> 00:06:14,471
Ask the API server whether it is healthy, and how many kinds of objects it knows:

78
00:06:16,233 --> 00:06:17,992
Let me try something.

79
00:06:17,992 --> 00:06:22,346
⚠️ This breaks a control-plane component on purpose.

80
00:06:22,346 --> 00:06:25,863
Do it only on your local Minikube cluster.

81
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:28,215
What should happen?

82
00:06:28,215 --> 00:06:29,456
The Pod runs.

83
00:06:29,456 --> 00:06:30,793
What happened?

84
00:06:30,793 --> 00:06:33,849
It is Pending, with NODE <none>:

85
00:06:33,849 --> 00:06:35,855
nobody picked a node.

86
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:42,499
Put the static Pod file back; the kubelet starts the scheduler again,

87
00:06:42,499 --> 00:06:45,410
and the scheduler places the waiting Pod:

88
00:06:47,167 --> 00:06:47,880
Got it.

89
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:50,631
What do I need to remember?

90
00:06:51,167 --> 00:06:52,656
Control plane:

91
00:06:52,656 --> 00:07:02,121
API server (front door), etcd (state), scheduler (where), controller manager (reconcile).

92
00:07:02,667 --> 00:07:03,693
Every node:

93
00:07:03,693 --> 00:07:12,281
kubelet (start containers), kube-proxy (Service networking), container runtime (containerd).

94
00:07:12,833 --> 00:07:18,428
Components watch the API server and act; a missing component stops one step:

95
00:07:18,428 --> 00:07:20,858
Pending without node → scheduler.

96
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:25,440
How does installing Minikube work?

97
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,541
Minikube creates a real Kubernetes cluster on your computer:

98
00:07:30,541 --> 00:07:33,871
one node, running inside a Docker container.

99
00:07:33,871 --> 00:07:41,288
It is the same Kubernetes as in production (the same API server, scheduler, kubelet…), just small.

100
00:07:41,288 --> 00:07:48,630
kubectl is the command-line client you use to talk to any Kubernetes cluster, including this one.

101
00:07:49,167 --> 00:07:57,233
You learn Kubernetes by using it, and you need a cluster you can break without consequences,

102
00:07:57,233 --> 00:08:01,793
without a cloud account and without paying anything.

103
00:08:01,793 --> 00:08:07,492
Minikube is that cluster; every lesson of this course runs on it.

104
00:08:07,492 --> 00:08:14,770
> ⚠️ Do not run the commands of this course against a production or shared cluster.

105
00:08:14,770 --> 00:08:20,381
They delete things, stop > components and break them on purpose.

106
00:08:22,133 --> 00:08:24,553
And how does it actually work?

107
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:32,693
Alternatives exist (kind, k3d, Docker Desktop's Kubernetes); they all give you a local cluster.

108
00:08:32,693 --> 00:08:40,845
This course uses Minikube everywhere, with one set of settings, so every output you see matches yours.

109
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:42,819
